# cuts/v1.0.30/cut.env -- the pins bin/rollforward_gate.sh binds for --cut v1.0.30
#
# The gate reads exactly TWO keys, and the mapping is DECLARED in the runner:
#     OSTLER_PIN_OA="$(_cut_pin DAEMON_COMMIT)"
#     OSTLER_PIN_CM051="$(_cut_pin CM051)"
# Everything else is prose the gate never touches. Keep it that way: the v1.0.24
# attempt to vendor OS003's fuller records dragged 464 lines of operator working
# notes, four private box IPs and five notarisation IDs into the product repo.
#
# WHY v1.0.30 EXISTS. THREE burnt tags. v1.0.27 and v1.0.28 died in the
# PREFLIGHT on the same exec-a-pinned-image defect in different files; #739
# closed that class. v1.0.29 PROVED IT: preflight PASSED for the first time,
# then it died inside make ship at check-orphans because CM051 #735 was written,
# unmerged and undeferred -- a real gate on a real condition. #735 is merged at
# 22892551e842 and this cuts from there. The v1.0.29 tag was NOT re-run: it
# points at ec770c9, which predates that merge, so the content genuinely would
# not be in the shipped ref. The payload is unchanged across all four tags.

# The daemon this DMG ships. UNCHANGED from v1.0.27, which never produced an
# artefact, so this is the first tag that will actually carry 0.4.57.
#
# hub-v0.4.57 is a LIGHTWEIGHT tag in ostler-ai/ostler-assistant: the ref points
# straight at COMMIT 723c682fd7b44b87e3769adfd2db2fdf59f5dadd, so the annotated
# tag-object trap v1.0.26 recorded does not apply here. Re-measured per tag
# rather than inherited, because the shape differs per tag and per repo.
#
# THE PIN NAMES THE SOURCE COMMIT, AND THAT DISTINCTION IS LOAD-BEARING. The two
# artefacts ship from DIFFERENT repositories: the daemon from
# ostler-ai/ostler-releases and the Hub app from ostler-ai/ostler-assistant. The
# hub-v0.4.57 tag in the RELEASES repo resolves to 14699971, a commit in that
# artefact-hosting repo, which is NOT the source commit and would name a tree the
# freshness gate cannot compare. The gate measures this pin against
# ostler-ai/ostler-assistant main.
#
# Cross-checked three ways: both published build-info.json files report commit
# 723c682f, and 723c682f is ostler-assistant main HEAD, so this pin is FRESH
# rather than merely less stale.
DAEMON_COMMIT=723c682fd7b4

# CM051 pin. The gate reads install.sh AT THIS REF:
#     repos/<CM051_REPO>/contents/install.sh?ref=$CM051_REF
# so the pin must name a tree whose install.sh is the one in the DMG.
#
# f219eb0 is the squash merge of #736, and it is STILL the most recent commit on
# main that touches install.sh -- #737 (the provenance gate fix) and this cut's
# version bump touch gui/, scripts/, tests/ and .github/, none of which this pin
# reads. That is why the key can be written here rather than re-pointed after the
# fact, and it was CHECKED rather than assumed.
#
# VERIFIED BY BLOB IDENTITY rather than by ancestry, because ancestry proves
# ordering and says nothing about content:
#     git rev-parse f219eb0:install.sh -> 689452950e35c9409be493be18a37505e1d76808
#     git rev-parse main:install.sh    -> 689452950e35c9409be493be18a37505e1d76808
#
# THE v1.0.27 LESSON THIS ENCODES: that key was re-pointed TWICE. Once because
# #711 landed after it was written, and once because the convergence merge
# re-pinned both wiki digests inside install.sh and moved the file again. A pin
# chosen early and trusted is a pin that goes stale while you are not looking.
#
# RE-VERIFY IMMEDIATELY BEFORE PUSHING THE TAG: compare the two blob shas above
# and re-point if they diverge.
CM051=f219eb0ff67f1a4c7c07486aea7779edf3e3289d
